{"id": "c0319c74-9e61-4ea8-8ba8-a5f1cc8815aa", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review your EBS setup in us-east-1 from two angles: volume configuration and data protection, and performance and monitoring. Then I'll give you a readiness verdict.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_FKOHjbnj9YOcXGh8ZgAjht", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-config-protection-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Assess EBS volume configuration and data protection for production readiness in us-east-1.",
  "context": "The user has a production launch next week and wants to know if their EBS storage is production-ready. This is a read-only review. Cover every AWS account associated with the agent space (use list_associations to find them). Use only non-mutating calls. Agent space account is 012345678901_1.",
  "resources": ["All EBS volumes, snapshots, and EBS-related settings in us-east-1 across all associated accounts"],
  "timeframe": "Current state, plus snapshot recency over the last 30 days",
  "criteria": "Report concisely: (1) Inventory: volume count, types (gp2/gp3/io1/io2/st1/sc1/standard), sizes, attached vs unattached/available volumes, and volumes with no recent attachment. (2) Encryption: unencrypted volumes and snapshots, whether account-level EBS encryption by default is enabled, and which KMS keys are used (default aws/ebs vs customer-managed). (3) Backup: volumes with no snapshot or no snapshot in the last 7 days; existence and state of Data Lifecycle Manager policies and AWS Backup plans/selections covering the volumes; whether snapshots are copied cross-region; Recycle Bin retention rules; snapshot public sharing (block public access setting). (4) Resilience and cost: gp2 volumes that could move to gp3, io1 that could move to io2, Multi-Attach usage, volumes with DeleteOnTermination on the root or data volumes of production instances, orphaned snapshots, fast snapshot restore config. (5) Tagging: volumes missing Name, Environment or Owner tags. Output a findings list with each item labeled Critical / Warning / OK, with volume or snapshot IDs where relevant, and keep the result compact (summarize large lists with counts plus the top examples)."
}", "preloaded_skills": ["discovering-topology"]}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS config, encryption, and backups in us-east-1"}, {"id": "tooluse_gEJxA2SSLLfmf0QWzSrnDb", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-performance-monitoring-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Assess EBS performance headroom and monitoring/alarm coverage for production readiness in us-east-1.",
  "context": "The user has a production launch next week and wants to know if their EBS storage is production-ready. This is a read-only review. Cover every AWS account associated with the agent space (use list_associations to find them). Use only non-mutating calls. Agent space account is 012345678901_1.",
  "resources": ["EBS volumes and attached EC2 instances in us-east-1, AWS/EBS and AWS/EC2 CloudWatch metrics, CloudWatch alarms"],
  "timeframe": "Last 14 days of metrics (ending 2026-11-11T19:13:00Z)",
  "criteria": "Report concisely: (1) Performance headroom: for attached volumes, compare peak VolumeReadOps/VolumeWriteOps (IOPS) and throughput against provisioned IOPS/throughput limits; flag gp2 volumes with BurstBalance low or trending down, volumes with high VolumeQueueLength, high VolumeIdleTime vs saturated volumes, and instances whose EBS-optimized status or instance-level EBS bandwidth/IOPS limits could bottleneck the volumes (check EBSIOBalance%/EBSByteBalance% for burstable-EBS instance types). Check for volumes currently in 'impaired' status or with pending volume modifications, and volume status checks. (2) Monitoring: whether CloudWatch alarms exist for EBS metrics (BurstBalance, VolumeQueueLength, VolumeStalledIOCheck, VolumeThroughputPercentage / VolumeConsumedReadWriteOps for io1/io2) and for disk-space usage via CloudWatch agent if present; whether alarms have actions (SNS) configured; any alarms currently in ALARM state. (3) Capacity: whether any filesystem-level disk usage metrics (CWAgent disk_used_percent) are available and show volumes above 80%. Output a findings list with each item labeled Critical / Warning / OK, with resource IDs, and keep the result compact (counts plus top examples for long lists)."
}", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics", "discovering-topology"]}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS performance headroom and alarms in us-east-1"}], "created_at": null}

